# Debian/Ubuntu apt packages — installed by tools/setup/linux.sh.
# Add a package by appending a line. Comments start with `#`.
#
# HOST TIERS. A row may carry `tier=<slim|standard|full>`; untagged means
# tier=slim, i.e. required on every host. linux.sh drops rows above the host's
# tier (loudly, naming the package and both tiers) and strips the token before
# calling apt-get — same mechanism macos.sh applies to manifests/brew, and the
# tagged rows here mirror brew's tier=standard set. Measured on ubuntu:24.04
# amd64 with universe enabled and --no-install-recommends (2026-08-21):
#   every row (tier=full/standard/slim)  388 packages   713.0 MiB
#   slim only (what ubuntu-slim gets)    149 packages   139.7 MiB
# The gap is concentrated in a handful of rows — agda +242 MiB, emscripten
# +150 MiB, r-base +57 MiB, llvm +53 MiB, pandoc +26 MiB, podman +21 MiB —
# which is why those are the ones tagged. Tagging a row that measures +0.0 MiB
# would look like cost control and be none.
#
# System-level packages only. Language runtimes (dotnet, python,
# java, bun) are pinned via `.mise.toml`, NOT via apt. Round 34
# migrated OpenJDK off apt onto mise.

build-essential
curl
ca-certificates
git

