# ── YubiKey / YubiHSM hardware-token substrate ────────────────────────────────
# Declarative per the maintainer's standing rule quoted in manifests/brew:
# "download whatever tools we need and link them in our declarative installers
# for all our OS shields". Hardware present on the maintainer's bench 2026-08-20:
# YubiHSM 2 (serial 39160506, fw 2.4.1) + 2x YubiKey 5 (fw 5.7.4).
#
# WHY LINUX NEEDS MORE THAN macOS HERE. macOS ships a CCID smartcard stack in the
# OS, so `brew install ykman` is sufficient there. Linux does not: without a
# running PC/SC daemon the CCID interface is invisible and ykman reports NO device
# even though it is plugged in and enumerated on USB. That is a silent
# half-working install -- the tool present, the device unreachable -- so pcscd and
# its client library are declared HERE rather than assumed from the runner image.
#
# udev: the yubikey-manager deb ships rules under /lib/udev/rules.d/ granting
# non-root access to the Yubico vendor id (0x1050). (The yubihsm-shell deb ships
# its own rules for the HSM, but that package is not declared here -- see below --
# so an HSM on a Linux host still needs those rules from Yubico's own repo.)
# Installing via apt is what puts them there; a pip/binary install of ykman does
# NOT, which is the usual reason a working macOS setup fails to reproduce on Linux.
#
# Verified installable on the runner 2026-08-20. Two corrections are recorded here
# because both are the same error in different disguises:
#
#   1. A first lookup returned 000 for four names. That is a FAILED REQUEST, not an
#      absent package; re-running returned 200. Reading a timeout as "not packaged"
#      is how a check that did not run gets treated as one that did.
#
#   2. yubihsm-shell WAS declared here on the strength of a 200 from
#      packages.ubuntu.com/noble, and CI then failed with
#      `E: Unable to locate package yubihsm-shell`. The 200 proved the package
#      exists IN THE DISTRO ARCHIVE. It did not prove it is installable FROM THE
#      SOURCES THIS RUNNER HAS CONFIGURED, which is the proposition that actually
#      had to hold. Verifying the weaker claim and treating it as the stronger one
#      is the whole failure mode -- so the entry is gone rather than papered over
#      with an apt-source change nobody asked for. It is now absent on BOTH
#      platforms (see manifests/brew), which is at least honest and symmetric:
#      the YubiHSM CLI is undeclared everywhere until it can be pinned properly.
#
# The four below are confirmed installable: CI installed them.
yubikey-manager   # ykman CLI — PIV/FIDO2/OpenPGP/YubiHSM-Auth applet management
yubico-piv-tool   # PIV slot + attestation tooling (hardware proof a key never left)
pcscd             # PC/SC smartcard daemon — REQUIRED for CCID; macOS has this built in
libpcsclite1      # PC/SC client library ykman links against

# ── OpenSC / pkcs11-tool (2026-08-20) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# OpenSC (`pkcs11-tool`), added 2026-08-20 with the macOS + Windows legs. This is
# the piece that makes the YubiHSM PKCS#11 module usable by something: the SDK
# ships yubihsm_pkcs11.so and, before this row, nothing in the declared toolchain
# could load a PKCS#11 module at all. It also drives a YubiKey PIV slot through
# OpenSC's own module, so it is the vendor-neutral half of this block.
#
# VERIFIED, and by the check the yubihsm-shell entry above failed. The lesson
# recorded above is that a 200 from packages.ubuntu.com proves a package exists in
# the DISTRO ARCHIVE, not that it is installable FROM THE SOURCES THIS RUNNER HAS.
# So this row was checked by parsing the archive's own binary-package indices and
# reading the COMPONENT each package sits in (2026-08-20):
#
#   dists/noble/universe/binary-amd64  opensc 0.25.0~rc1-1build2   (64,754 pkgs parsed)
#   dists/jammy/universe/binary-amd64  opensc 0.22.0-1ubuntu2      (58,824 pkgs parsed)
#   dists/noble/main/binary-amd64      opensc ABSENT               ( 6,099 pkgs parsed)
#
# universe, not main -- and universe is exactly the component yubikey-manager,
# yubico-piv-tool and pcscd above already come from, all three of which CI has
# installed successfully. That is the entailment this row rests on: same suite,
# same component, same runner sources. Both Ubuntu releases linux.sh supports
# carry it, so no jammy alias mapping is needed (contrast libicu74 -> libicu70).
#
# /usr/bin/pkcs11-tool ships in the `opensc` binary package itself, not in
# `opensc-pkcs11` (that one is the .so). Confirmed from the package's own file
# list; `opensc-pkcs11` is a dependency and is pulled in automatically.
#
# COST, stated rather than assumed. This is +1 package on all 24 gate jobs,
# uncached, forever -- the most expensive shelf in the repo (install-v2 caches
# only $HOME; nothing apt writes to /usr survives). A CI runner has no HSM and
# no YubiKey, so the CI benefit is zero. It is landed anyway because the four
# rows above already made exactly that trade for exactly this hardware, and
# refusing the fifth on cost while keeping the four would be a razor applied to
# the cheapest row instead of the decision.
#
# The DEBT this paragraph filed -- "the right fix is a tier= gate for apt, which
# does not exist yet" -- is PAID as of 081M0K36K69087G0R003BYSCF8: linux.sh now
# honours `tier=`. This row is deliberately left UNTAGGED anyway. opensc's
# marginal download on top of the slim set measures +0.0 MiB (it is already
# pulled in as a dependency), so tagging it would buy nothing and would only
# make the manifest read as if the cost had been addressed. The rows that were
# tagged are the ones that were measured to be expensive.
opensc            # pkcs11-tool — vendor-neutral PKCS#11 CLI; module loader for yubihsm_pkcs11.so

# ── YubiHSM 2 SDK: STILL UNDECLARED ON DEBIAN/UBUNTU (2026-08-20) ─────────────
# Re-verified today by the index parse above, so the removal recorded earlier is
# now backed by the strong check rather than by a CI failure:
#
#   dists/noble/universe   yubihsm-shell ABSENT · yubihsm-connector ABSENT · libyubihsm2 ABSENT
#   dists/noble/main       (none of the three)
#   dists/jammy/universe   yubihsm-shell ABSENT
#
# There is genuinely no apt route. Yubico ships
# https://developers.yubico.com/YubiHSM2/Releases/yubihsm2-sdk-2026-04-ubuntu2404-amd64.tar.gz
# (+ a detached .sig) -- a TARBALL OF .debs, not a .deb. No mechanism here fits:
#   from-deb   takes ONE .deb url, performs NO checksum and NO signature check
#              (bare `dpkg -i`), and its binary-only idempotence check cannot
#              install the library-only yubihsm-pkcs11 package at all. Using the
#              repo's one unverified mechanism to install an HSM driver would
#              invert our own standard at the point it matters most.
#   from-url   downloads to a path and stops. It cannot untar or dpkg anything.
#
# AND AN APT ROW WOULD BE THE WRONG SHAPE EVEN IF ONE EXISTED. linux.sh skips apt
# entirely on NixOS (the /etc/NIXOS marker), and the cluster nodes -- the only
# machines that will hold an HSM -- are NixOS. So an apt entry would cost 24 gate
# jobs every run forever and reach zero nodes. The pinned nixpkgs already carries
# yubihsm-connector 3.0.7 and yubihsm-shell 2.7.3, source-built and hash-pinned.
#
# TODO, owned by workitem 081M0B5V6Z5087G0R0026RANJ3, BLOCKED on maintainer
# sign-off (Q1-Q7 + Q9 in the design doc). What must be supplied before Linux can
# be declarative for the HSM:
#   1. Answers to Q2 (which SDK pieces land on a node; yubihsm-setup excluded?),
#      Q4 (yubihsm-shell CLI on nodes at all?) and Q5 (all nodes or HSM-holders).
#   2. Then full-ai-cluster/nixos/modules/yubihsm.nix -- NOT a tools/setup row:
#      the connector daemon, a yubihsm-connector system user, a systemd unit
#      (nixpkgs ships no services.yubihsm-connector module), and
#      services.udev.extraRules for 1050:0030 with OWNER="yubihsm-connector".
#      The udev rule is REQUIRED: a YubiHSM 2 is a USB BULK device, invisible to
#      pcscd/OpenSC/ykman, and without the rule the node device is root-only --
#      which presents as a healthy daemon that cannot see a plugged-in HSM.
#      The 1050:0030 constant is Yubico's own, read from deb/70-yubihsm-connector.rules
#      at tag 3.0.7, not inferred.
